            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Weekly Observation: Why 'Can Run' Doesn't Mean 'Can Sell'</h2>
<p>This week, several e-commerce accounts we managed showed a shared symptom: creatives passed review smoothly, CTR was decent, but CPA doubled. After reviewing each creative line by line, we found the issue wasn't traffic acquisition—it was the missing 'persuasion logic' in the creative itself.</p>
<p>'Can run' means the creative passed Meta's policy review, got impressions, and generated clicks. 'Can sell' means the user took the next step—added to cart, clicked checkout, or stayed on page for more than 30 seconds.</p>
<p>Between these two goals lies a three-layer conversion funnel.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Three Dimensions to Distinguish Running from Selling</h2>
<h3>1. Intent Matching</h3>
<p>Creatives that can run often have 'strong visual impact' and 'complete feature showcases,' but that doesn't equal 'user intent matching.'</p>
<p>Review checklist:<br />
- Does the first frame convey core value within 1.5 seconds?<br />
- Does the copy pain point correspond to the user's real search intent?<br />
- Does the CTA match the user's current decision stage?</p>
<p>An App in the utility category launched a batch of 'feature demo + download now' creatives with 4% CTR but only 12% Day-1 retention. After switching to scenario-based openings like 'solving XX problem' with a '3-step' progress bar, retention climbed to 38%. The issue wasn't creative quality—it was intent matching.</p>
<h3>2. Trust-Building Speed</h3>
<p>Creatives that can run often overlook trust signals. Meta users swipe at an average speed of 1.7 seconds. If the creative can't build credibility quickly, users skip.</p>
<p>This week's observation: creatives with genuine user testimonials, real-photo scenarios, or brand credentials showed 1.8-2.3x higher CVR than pure product renders. Especially in beauty and utility categories, users are highly sensitive to 'authenticity.'</p>
<h3>3. Action Path Fluency</h3>
<p>Creatives that can run often stop at 'generate interest' without providing a clear action path.</p>
<p>Common issues:<br />
- CTA only says 'Learn More' but the landing page doesn't link to the corresponding module<br />
- Promotional info shown in creative but not updated on the landing page<br />
- Video creatives showcase more than 5 selling points—users can't remember the core message</p>
<hr />
<h2>This Week's Review Recommendations: 3 Steps</h2>
<p><strong>Step 1: Break Down Creative Attribution Dimensions</strong></p>
<p>Don't just look at CTR and CPC. Add these metrics comparison at the ad set level:<br />
- Creative level: CTR, Video View Rate, 3-second completion rate<br />
- Landing page level: Page Engagement Rate, Bounce Rate<br />
- Conversion level: CVR, CPA, ROAS</p>
<p>Only when all three indicators hit targets can a creative truly 'sell.'</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Establish Creative Classification Standards</strong></p>
<p>Classify by conversion contribution:<br />
- Type A: Can run + can sell, ROAS &gt; 1.2x target, priority for scaling<br />
- Type B: Can run + weak conversion, high CTR but CVR below average by 30%, needs landing page optimization<br />
- Type C: Only can run, maintains account activity through volume but not a primary investment</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Watch for Policy Gray Area Signals</strong></p>
<p>Meta tightened audits on 'limited-time promotions' and 'countdown' elements this week. Some previously runnable creatives were mistakenly flagged. Prepare alternative versions for Type B creatives to avoid campaign gaps during adjustments.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Bottom Line</h2>
<p>Creatives that can run are the baseline; those that can sell are the goal. This week's observations tell us: with Q4 traffic costs rising, continuing to nurture creatives with a 'can run is enough' mindset wastes budget. We recommend all campaign teams complete a creative classification round this week, decisively pause Type C creatives, and concentrate budget on Type A and optimized Type B creatives.</p>
<p>For further discussion on account review details, feel free to contact the Yiyu Digital Media team.</p>
